course: dictionary
lesson: 8918
title: Sidewalk or pavement?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words change from place to place. «When you hear a siren near a road, stay on the pavement or sidewalk and wait until the vehicle has passed.» The words around sidewalk tell you where to be: beside the road, out of the way of the traffic.
explain: The writer puts two words together, pavement and sidewalk, so you can check their meaning. Pavement is the British word, and sidewalk is the American one. Both name the same path. To test it, swap in the word path: «stay on the path beside the road» still makes sense.
explain: «A girl in Colorado meets a strange cat on the sidewalk.» Colorado is in America, so the sidewalk here is the American word for the path where people walk beside the road.
fact: A sidewalk is an American word for the path at the side of a road where people walk.
fact: In Britain and Australia the same path is called a pavement or footpath.
fact: Stay on the sidewalk, away from the road, to keep safe.
fact: Cross the street only at corners or crossings.
fact: Children can draw with sidewalk chalk, but the picture is still on the path, not in the road.
exercise: When you are told to stay on the sidewalk, where should you stay? | Under a parked car | In the middle of the road | On the roof of a house | *On the path beside the road
exercise: In Britain, what would you call a sidewalk? | Roundabout | Lift | Cookie | *Pavement
exercise: Where should you cross the street? | *At a corner or crossing | In the middle of a busy road | Between parked cars | Wherever you like
test: «A girl in Colorado meets a strange cat on the sidewalk.» Where is the girl? | Inside a shop | *On the path beside the road | On a farm | Up a tree
test: Which word means the same as sidewalk in Britain? | *Footpath | Road | Driveway | Elevator
exam: «British biscuit, pavement and lift are American cookie, sidewalk and elevator.» Which American word means the same as the British pavement? | Road | Elevator | Cookie | *Sidewalk
